Mexican immigrant women&#x02019;s needs? Cancer Control,12(Suppl 2), 42&#x02013;50. <pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16327750</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="CR15"><label>15.</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Meade</surname><given-names>CD</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Cancer, culture and literacy: Critical next steps in improving care for diverse populations</article-title><source>Cancer Control</source><year>2005</year><volume>12</volume><issue>Suppl 2</issue><fpage>4</fpage><lpage>5</lpage></citation><citation citation-type="display-unstructured">Meade, C. D. (2005). Cancer, culture and literacy: Critical next steps in improving care for diverse populations. Cancer Control,12(Suppl 2), 4&#x02013;5. <pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16327744</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="CR16"><label>16.</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Calderon</surname><given-names>JL</given-names></name><name><surname>Morales</surname><given-names>LS</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>Variation in the readability of items within surveys</article-title><source>American Journal of Medical Quality</source><year>2006</year><volume>21</volume><issue>1</issue><fpage>49</fpage><lpage>56</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1177/1062860605283572</pub-id></citation><citation citation-type="display-unstructured">Calderon, J. L., Morales, L. S., et al. (2006). Variation in the readability of items within surveys. American Journal of Medical Quality,21(1), 49&#x02013;56. <pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16401705</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="CR17"><label>17.</label><citation citation-type="other">Estey, A., Musseau, A., et al. (1991). Comprehension levels of patients reading health information. <italic>Patient Education Counseling,</italic><italic>18</italic>, 165&#x02013;169. (1996), <italic>30</italic>, 205&#x02013;208.</citation></ref><ref id="CR18"><label>18.</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Davis</surname><given-names>TC</given-names></name><name><surname>Crouch</surname><given-names>MA</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>The gap between patient reading comprehension and the readability of patient education materials</article-title><source>Journal of Family Practice</source><year>1990</year><volume>31</volume><issue>5</issue><fpage>533</fpage><lpage>538</lpage></citation><citation citation-type="display-unstructured">Davis, T. C., Crouch, M. A., et al. (1990). The gap between patient reading comprehension and the readability of patient education materials. Journal of Family Practice,31(5), 533&#x02013;538. <pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">2230677</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="CR19"><label>19.</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Miller</surname><given-names>B</given-names></name><name><surname>Bodie</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Determination of reading comprehension level for effective patient health-education materials</article-title><source>Nursing Research</source><year>1994</year><volume>43</volume><issue>2</issue><fpage>118</fpage><lpage>119</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1097/00006199-199403000-00013</pub-id></citation><citation citation-type="display-unstructured">Miller, B., &#x00026; Bodie, M. (1994). Determination of reading comprehension level for effective patient health-education materials. Nursing Research,43(2), 118&#x02013;119. <pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8152936</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="CR20"><label>20.</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Freda</surname><given-names>MC</given-names></name><name><surname>Damus</surname><given-names>K</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>Evaluation of the readability of ACOG patient education pamphlets. The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists</article-title><source>Obstetrics and Gynecology</source><year>1999</year><volume>93</volume><issue>5 Pt 1</issue><fpage>771</fpage><lpage>774</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1016/S0029-7844(98)00518-3</pub-id></citation><citation citation-type="display-unstructured">Freda, M. C., Damus, K., et al. (1999). Evaluation of the readability of ACOG patient education pamphlets. The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists. Obstetrics and Gynecology,93(5 Pt 1), 771&#x02013;774. <pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10912984</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="CR21"><label>21.</label><citation citation-type="other">Rogers, E. S., Spalding, S. L., et al. (2008). Are patient-administered attention deficit hyperactivity disorder scales suitable for adults? <italic>Journal of Attention Disorders</italic>. doi:10.1177/1087054708323017</citation></ref><ref id="CR22"><label>22.</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Wallace</surname><given-names>LS</given-names></name><name><surname>Keenum</surname><given-names>AJ</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>Readability and cognitive complexity of self-administered opioid assessment screening tools</article-title><source>Journal of Opioid Management</source><year>2007</year><volume>3</volume><issue>6</issue><fpage>338</fpage><lpage>344</lpage></citation><citation citation-type="display-unstructured">Wallace, L. S., Keenum, A. J., et al. (2007). Readability and cognitive complexity of self-administered opioid assessment screening tools. Journal of Opioid Management,3(6), 338&#x02013;344. <pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">18290586</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="CR23"><label>23.</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Fryback</surname><given-names>DG</given-names></name><name><surname>Dunham</surname><given-names>NC</given-names></na